{% markdown %}

[Datatype](https://franktisellano.github.io/datatype/), by Frank Tisellano, is a variable font that turns text into charts. Write `{b:30,70,50,90}` in any element and the font renders an inline bar chart through OpenType ligature substitution. There are also sparklines (`{l:...}`) and pie charts (`{p:...}`), with weight and width axes adjusting density and visual weight. The rendering is the typesetting.

Variable fonts have been getting steadily more interesting, and this is one of the more delightful uses I've come across. Data visualization, normally a JavaScript concern, gets pushed into the font layer. That's the same instinct behind a lot of design system work I find interesting, where the answer to "do we need another component" sometimes turns out to be "no, the platform already has a primitive that does this." I've been circling this idea for a while: back in 2018 I sketched [a hypothetical web font for life-sciences data](/posts/20180319-data-font-for-life-sciences.html), wondering whether a font could carry domain meaning. Datatype is that instinct, executed by someone with the typography skill to actually pull it off.

## Datatype on real data: my own blog cadence

To see how this feels in practice, here's some real data about this blog rendered through Datatype. The values in curly braces are literal text in the HTML; the font is doing the drawing. If the font fails to load, you'll see the raw expressions instead, which is itself a useful preview of the fallback behavior.

{% endmarkdown %}

